{
  "id": "maximum-product-subarray",
  "questionId": "maximum-product-subarray",
  "slug": "maximum-product-subarray",
  "title": "Maximum Product Subarray",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Dp 1d",
  "patterns": [
    "dynamic programming",
    "kadane's algorithm"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Maximum Subarray only needed a running max, because adding a number never flips the sign of a good running sum - does multiplying behave the same way?",
    "what happens to the best running product so far if the next number is negative and the running product was also negative?",
    "at each element, the best product ending here could come from extending the previous max, extending the previous min (if this number is negative), or starting fresh at this element",
    "track both cur_max and cur_min at each step: new_max = max(num, num*cur_max, num*cur_min), new_min = min(num, num*cur_max, num*cur_min), and update the global best from new_max"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "only tracking a running max product, missing that a very negative running min can become the new max when multiplied by another negative",
    "forgetting to reset both running max and min to the current number when a zero is encountered",
    "updating cur_max using the already-updated cur_min (or vice versa) instead of computing both from the previous step's values"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of integers `nums`, which may include negative\nnumbers and zeros.\n\n**Find the contiguous subarray of `nums` (at least one element) whose product is the largest possible, and return that product.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 2 * 10^4",
      "-10 <= nums[i] <= 10",
      "the product of any prefix or suffix of nums fits in a 32-bit integer"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single negative number that flips the running max/min when multiplied by another negative",
      "a zero in the middle of the array that should reset the running product",
      "all negative numbers with an odd count (the best slice must drop one negative from an end)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [2, 3, -2, 4]",
        "output": "6",
        "expectedOutput": "6",
        "explanation": "the slice [2, 3] has the largest product, 6"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [-2, 0, -1]",
        "output": "0",
        "expectedOutput": "0",
        "explanation": "the only nonzero slices contain a single -2 or -1, both worse than the slice [0]"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [-2, 3, -4]",
        "output": "24",
        "expectedOutput": "24",
        "explanation": "the entire array multiplies to (-2) * 3 * (-4) = 24, since the two negatives cancel out"
      }
    ],
